 ## Overview
-DIB-R is a differentiable rendering technique for meshes that computes exact gradients of the output image with respect to input parameters by analytically differentiating the rendering pipeline, specifically addressing the non-differentiability of standard rasterization [S1, unofficial][S2]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Neural 3D Mesh Renderer (NMR)](7042-neural-3d-mesh-renderer-nmr.md) by explicitly separating the image into foreground and background regions to handle gradients differently, thereby avoiding the blurriness associated with soft rasterization while mitigating vanishing gradients in background areas [S1, unofficial][S2].
+DIB-R is a differentiable rendering technique for meshes that computes exact gradients of the output image with respect to input parameters by analytically differentiating the rendering pipeline, specifically addressing the non-differentiable nature of standard rasterization [S1, unofficial][S2]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Neural 3D Mesh Renderer (NMR)](7042-neural-3d-mesh-renderer-nmr.md) by explicitly separating the image into foreground and background regions to handle gradients differently, avoiding the blurriness associated with soft rasterization while maintaining differentiability [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 ## How it works
-DIB-R divides the image plane into two distinct regions: foreground pixels (covered by at least one mesh face) and background pixels (uncovered by any face) [S1, unofficial][S2]. The method computes gradients for these regions using different mechanisms to ensure continuous flow of error signals [S1, unofficial][S2].
+DIB-R divides pixels into two distinct categories: foreground pixels (covered by at least one mesh face) and background pixels (uncovered by any face) [S1, unofficial][S2]. This separation allows the method to apply analytical gradients where the visibility is deterministic and probabilistic/distance-based aggregation where it is not [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** 3D mesh vertices, face connectivity, vertex attributes, camera parameters, and optionally a reference alpha mask [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Outputs:** Rendered image, alpha channel, and gradients with respect to vertices and attributes [S1, unofficial][S2].
+### Inputs and Outputs
+*   **Inputs:** 3D mesh geometry (vertices, faces), vertex attributes (e.g., color, normals), camera parameters (intrinsics, extrinsics), and optionally a reference alpha mask [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Outputs:** Differentiable rendered image and gradients with respect to mesh vertices and attributes [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
-**Data Structures:**
